14. g3 {and now the game continues:} Qe7 15. Bc4 Kg7 16. Qf3 Rf8 17. Qe4 Nd7
18. O-O-O Nf6 19. Qf4 d5 20. Be2 Bxe6 21. Bf3 c4 22. Qd4 Qa3+ 23. Kb1 Rad8 24.
g4 Rd6 25. Rd2 Rb6+ 26. Kc2 Kg8 27. Kd1 Rb1+ 28. Ke2 Rb2 29. Nh3 Ne4 30. Bxe4
Bxg4+ 31. Ke1 dxe4 32. Nf4 Rxd2 33. Qxd2 Qc5 34. Rg1 Rxf4 35. Rxg4 Rxg4 36.
Qd8+ Qf8 37. Qd5+ Qf7 {- it's possible - JS}) 14. Bc4 Kg7 ({It seems both
players and the annotator missed} 14... Qh4+ {winning the B on c4 - JS}) 15.
Qf3 Rf8 (15... Qh4+ {still wins! JS}) 16. Qe4 Nd7 17. O-O-O Nf6 18. Qf4 d5 19.
Be2 Bxe6 20. Bf3 c4 21. Qd4 Qa3+ 22. Kb1 Rad8 23. g4 Rd6 24. Rd2 Rb6+ 25. Kc2
Kg8 26. Kd1 Rb1+ 27. Ke2 Rb2 28. Nh3 Ne4 29. Bxe4 Bxg4+ 30. Ke1 dxe4 31. Nf4
Rxd2 32. Qxd2 Qc5 33. Rg1 Rxf4 34. Rxg4 Rxg4 35. Qd8+ Qf8 36. Qd5+ Qf7 {
Source: Birmingham Daily Post, 27 April 1955 - BH Wood column; note - CHESS,
30 April 1955, p320, shows the result of game as if the colours were the other
way round. However, another source, the Manchester Guardian, 21 April 1955,
shows that Lloyd was White and this is also consistent with the pairings as
otherwise the two players would both have had the same colour for three rounds
in a row - JS} 0-1

